From: lmoresi <louis.moresi@anu.edu.au>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 19:30:01 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] The pressure sub-solve's iteration cap is the user's on
 both Stokes paths

- Setting strategy wrote fieldsplit_pressure_ksp_max_it and
  fieldsplit_velocity_ksp_max_it back to 200 over the user's values. Both are
  now written by _push_default_option, which leaves a value it did not write
  (set on petsc_options or on the command line) alone. Its record is its own,
  so the multigrid bundle's test of which keys are the user's is unchanged.
  Every writer of these keys writes 200; a user value of exactly 200 is not
  told apart from the default, which is then harmless.
- The rotated free-slip path builds its own Krylov solver under a per-solve
  prefix and fixed its pressure cap at 200, out of the user's reach; on P2/dP1
  triangle meshes the cap could not be raised. It now reads the solver's own
  fieldsplit_pressure_ksp_max_it (default 200) from the options database, so
  the same option reaches both paths.
- rotated-freeslip.md says so, and that the cached solver takes the cap set
  before the first solve.

Left open: the sub-solves' rtol and ksp_type are still rewritten by strategy.

Default unchanged: a probe of a standard, a standard-with-strategy and a
rotated P2/dP1 solve gives identical solution bytes and iteration counts;
every SNES and KSP solve in test_1111, test_1087 and test_1018 (221) matches a
development run exactly, apart from two rotated solves whose last residual
takes one of two values from session to session on development as well.

test_0908 (tier B): a user's caps survive a later strategy; a strategy alone
keeps 200; the rotated pressure sub-solver carries the user's cap. The first
and third fail before the change.

Underworld development team with AI support from Claude Code
